<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best all-mountain skis for most people have a waist width around 85–100 mm, some rocker in the tip, and a length between your chin and the top of your head, depending on ability. Narrower skis carve better on groomers; wider ones float better in soft snow. Use the <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> to find your length.</p></div>

<h2>What "all-mountain" means</h2>
<p>All-mountain skis are built to handle a bit of everything: groomed runs, bumps, crud, and the occasional powder day. They're the right choice if you want one pair of skis for most conditions. The trade-off is that a specialist ski does any one thing better, but the all-mountain ski does many things well.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Waist width</h2>
<p>Waist width is the width of the ski underfoot, in millimeters. It's the biggest factor in how a ski behaves.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Under about 85 mm:</strong> quick edge to edge and grippy on hard snow. Best if you mostly ski groomers, especially in the East.</li>
  <li><strong>About 85–100 mm:</strong> the classic all-mountain range. Versatile enough for groomers and soft snow.</li>
  <li><strong>About 100 mm and up:</strong> more float in powder and crud, but slower to turn on hardpack. Better for western resorts that get frequent storms.</li>
</ul>
<p>Think about where you ski most. If your home mountain is often icy, go narrower. If you chase storms out West, go wider.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Length</h2>
<p>A common starting point is a ski that stands somewhere between your chin and the top of your head. Then adjust:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Shorter</strong> if you're a beginner, prefer short turns, or are lighter for your height.</li>
  <li><strong>Longer</strong> if you're advanced, ski fast, ski a lot of powder, or are heavier for your height.</li>
  <li><strong>Rocker matters:</strong> skis with a lot of tip and tail rocker ski shorter than their length, so you can size up slightly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <%= link_to "ski size chart and calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> gives you a length range from your height, weight, and ability.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Profile (camber and rocker)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Camber:</strong> an arch underfoot that gives edge grip and rebound on hard snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Tip rocker:</strong> the tip rises earlier, which helps the ski turn easily and float in soft snow. Most all-mountain skis have it.</li>
  <li><strong>Tip and tail rocker:</strong> even more playful and forgiving, at the cost of some grip on ice.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most modern all-mountain skis use rocker in the tip and camber underfoot, which is a good default.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Turn radius and stiffness</h2>
<p>Turn radius, listed in meters, describes the ski's natural turn shape. A shorter radius likes quick, short turns; a longer radius likes big, fast arcs. Stiffness matters too: softer skis are forgiving and easy at slower speeds, while stiffer skis are stable at speed but demand more strength and technique.</p>

<h2>Match the ski to your ability</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Beginner to intermediate:</strong> a softer, narrower <%= gear_link "all-mountain ski", "all mountain skis" %> with tip rocker and a shorter length. Or keep renting while you learn; see <%= link_to "rent or buy ski gear", guide_path("rent-or-buy-ski-gear") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Intermediate to advanced:</strong> a medium-stiff ski in the 85–100 mm range, sized near your height.</li>
  <li><strong>Advanced, storm skiing:</strong> a wider, longer ski, or a two-ski quiver with a dedicated <%= gear_link "powder ski", "powder skis" %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bindings and boots</h2>
<p>Most skis are sold flat (no bindings) or with a system binding. Bindings must be mounted and adjusted by a shop to your boot sole length, weight, height, and ability, which sets the release value. It's a safety setting, so don't skip it. Shop <%= gear_link "ski bindings", "ski bindings" %> with your skis if they come flat.</p>
<p>Boots matter even more than skis. A ski that's perfect on paper won't feel right in a sloppy boot. See our guide to <%= link_to "choosing ski boots", guide_path("best-ski-boots") %> before you spend on skis.</p>

<h2>Try before you buy</h2>
<p>Many resorts and shops offer demo skis you can rent for a day, often with the fee credited toward a purchase. Demoing two or three widths on the same day is the fastest way to learn what you like. Check <%= link_to "where it's snowing this week", where_is_it_snowing_path %> and plan a demo day around good conditions.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "What width should all-mountain skis be?", answer: "Around 85 to 100 mm underfoot suits most skiers. Go narrower if you mostly ski groomed or icy snow, and wider if you ski a lot of powder." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "How long should my all-mountain skis be?", answer: "Usually between your chin and the top of your head. Go shorter if you're a beginner or prefer short turns, and longer if you're advanced, heavier, or ski fast." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Are all-mountain skis good for beginners?", answer: "Yes. A softer, narrower all-mountain ski with tip rocker is forgiving and easy to turn, and it grows with you as you improve." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Do skis come with bindings?", answer: "Some do, as a system binding; others are sold flat. Either way, a shop should mount and adjust the bindings to your boots, weight, and ability." } %>
